<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=woodblock-printing">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woodblock printing</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as wood-block printing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woodblock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> is a technique of printing text or images by carving them in relief on a wooden block, inking it and pressing paper onto it. It developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and was in use under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>, which produced some of the earliest surviving printed texts, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a> of 868. Accounts of the Tang Dynasty treat printing as one of the period's significant technological developments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodblock-printing.jpg" alt="Woodblock printing" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Library · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jingangjing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Woodblock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> is a technique used widely throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and originating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in antiquity, first as a method of printing on textiles and later on paper. Each page or image is made by carving a wooden block so that only some areas and lines remain at the original level; these are inked and show in the print. Carving the blocks is skilled and laborious work, but a large number of impressions can then be printed. The earliest surviving examples on cloth from China date to before 220 CE, and printing existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> by the 7th century.</p><p>Inscribed seals of metal or stone, especially jade, and inscribed stone tablets probably inspired the invention. Copies of classical texts on tablets were set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> for scholars to copy, and ink-squeeze rubbings recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> bibliography are believed to have led to early duplication of texts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, the Directorate of education and other agencies used block prints to disseminate standard versions of the Classics, histories, philosophical works, encyclopaedias and books on medicine and war. From 932 to 955 the Twelve Classics were printed. In 971 work began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> on the complete Buddhist canon, which needed 130,000 blocks, took ten years to carve and was printed in 983. Block <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> made books cheaper, and by the eleventh century their price had fallen to about a tenth. Even so, the historian Endymion Wilkinson notes that it never supplanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, which remained dominant in imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and elite scholars often preferred them.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> spread early. The Great Dharani Sutra, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> in 1966, dates between 704 and 751. In Japan Empress Shōtoku ordered a million copies of a dhāraṇī sutra around 770. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> the Goryeo Daejanggyeong, carved again from 1237 after the first blocks burned in the Mongol invasion of 1232, was finished in 1248 with 81,258 blocks. In Japan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, woodblock printing produced illustrated books and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a> prints.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Woodblock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> by 1000 CE. The Islamic world used a block technique called ṭarsh from 900 to 1444, mostly for protective amulets, and it reached Muslim Spain in the 10th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> it became chiefly a method of printing textiles. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the technique was used for textile patterns by the mid-14th century and for images on sheets by the end of that century, and block books appeared in the mid-15th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> it remained the most common way of printing books until the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Woodblock printing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Woodblock printing</b> developed during the Tang and was used chiefly for Buddhist texts and calendars; the Diamond Sutra of 868, found at Dunhuang, is the oldest surviving dated printed book, though the commercial potential of printing would not be fully realised until the Song.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>before 220 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>These were prints on silk; the earliest prints on paper date from about 650–670, and the Diamond Sutra of 868 is the oldest dated printed book.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking<span>179–141 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">Paper money<span>1023</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">Movable type<span>c. 1040</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=woodblock-printing">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
